@Tutorial(time: 15) {
    @Intro(title: "Killing the player") {
        We can kill enemies by jumping on them, but the player still can't die.
        Let's fix this.

        We want to detect being hit by an enemy differently from squashing them.
        We want the player to die when they're moving on the floor, but not if
        they're in the air. We could use vector math to distinguish the two kinds
        of collisions. Instead, though, we will use an
        [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Farea3d.html#class-area3d)
        node, which works well for hitboxes.
    }
    @Section(title: "Hitbox with the Area node") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Head back to the `player.tscn` scene and add a new child node
            [Area3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Farea3d.html#class-area3d).
            Name it `MobDetector`. Add a
            [CollisionShape3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcollisionshape3d.html#class-collisionshape3d)
            node as a child of it.

            @Image(source: 01.adding_area_node.webp)

            In the _Inspector_, assign a cylinder shape to it.

            @Image(source: 02.cylinder_shape.webp)

            Here is a trick you can use to make the collisions only happen when
            the player is on the ground or close to it. You can reduce the
            cylinder's height and move it up to the top of the character. This
            way, when the player jumps, the shape will be too high up for the
            enemies to collide with it.

            @Image(source: 03.cylinder_in_editor.png)

            You also want the cylinder to be wider than the sphere. This way,
            the player gets hit before colliding and being pushed on top of the
            monster's collision box.

            The wider the cylinder, the more easily the player will get killed.

            Next, select the `MobDetector` node again, and in the _Inspector_,
            turn **off** its _Monitorable_ property. This makes it so other
            physics nodes cannot detect the area. The complementary _Monitoring_
            property allows it to detect collisions. Then, remove the _Collision
            -> Layer_ and set the mask to the "enemies" layer.

            @Image(source: 04.mob_detector_properties.webp)

            When areas detect a collision, they emit signals. We're going to
            connect one to the `Player` node. Select `MobDetector` and go to the
            Signals dock, double-click the `body_entered` signal and connect it
            to the `Player`

            @Image(source: 05.body_entered_signal.webp)

            The _MobDetector_ will emit `body_entered` when a
            [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
            or a
            [RigidBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Frigidbody3d.html#class-rigidbody3d)
            node enters it. As it only masks the "enemies" physics layers, it
            will only detect the `Mob` nodes.

            Code-wise, we're going to do two things: emit a signal we'll later
            use to end the game and destroy the player. We can wrap these
            operations in a `die()` function that helps us put a descriptive
            label on the code.

            @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-09.gd, previousFile: player-08.gd)
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Ending the game") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            We can use the `Player`'s `hit` signal to end the game. All we need
            to do is connect it to the `Main` node and stop the `MobTimer` in
            reaction.

            Open `main.tscn`, select the `Player` node, and in the _Signals_
            dock, connect its `hit` signal to the `Main` node.

            @Image(source: 06.player_hit_signal.webp)

            Get the timer, and stop it, in the `_on_player_hit()` function.

            @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-03.gd, previousFile: main-02.gd)

            If you try the game now, the monsters will stop spawning when you die,
            and the remaining ones will leave the screen.

            Notice also that the game no longer crashes or displays an error when
            the player dies. Because we are stopping the MobTimer, it no longer
            triggers the `_on_mob_timer_timeout()` function.

            Also note that the enemy colliding with the player and dying depends
            on the size and position of the `Player` and the `Mob`'s collision
            shapes. You may need to move them and resize them to achieve a tight
            game feel.

            You can pat yourself on the back: you prototyped a complete 3D game,
            even if it's still a bit rough.

            From there, we'll add a score, the option to retry the game, and
            you'll see how you can make the game feel much more alive with
            minimalistic animations.
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Code checkpoint") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Here are the complete scripts for the `Main`, `Mob`, and `Player`
            nodes, for reference. You can use them to compare and check your code.

            Starting with `main.gd`.

            @Code(name: "main.gd", file: main-03.gd)

            Next is `mob.gd`.

            @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-05.gd)

            Finally, the longest script, `player.gd`:

            @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-09.gd)

            See you in the next lesson to add the score and the retry option.
        }
        @Steps {
        }
    }
}
